1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Welcome back. Every extra turn an agent loop takes costs more tokens, adds latency, and gives errors

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
another chance to compound. Reach for that power only when you need it, and don't pay for it when

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
you don't need it. This lesson covers the trade-offs you weigh before you build. Let's break it down.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:25,000
When you weigh an agent against a workflow, it comes down to three axes. The first is latency.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
A loop makes several model calls in sequence, so a user waits longer than they would for a single

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
response. The second is token cost. Every turn resends the growing context, so the bill climbs

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
with each step the agent takes. The third is task performance. This is what you're buying with the

8
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
other two. The model's ability to adapt, handle messier inputs, and reach outcomes a rigid script

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:58,000
couldn't. Anthropic frames it plainly. Agentic systems trade latency and cost for better task

10
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:04,000
performance. The whole decision is deciding whether that trade pays off for the job in front of you.

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
Here's the core trade-off stated as a straight comparison. A deterministic workflow gives you

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
predictability and consistency. It's cheaper, it's faster, and it does the same thing on every run,

13
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
which makes it easy to test and easy to trust. A model-driven agent gives you flexibility. It

14
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:33,000
handles tasks you couldn't fully script, and it adapts when inputs surprise you. But that autonomy

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
costs more, runs slower, and opens the door to mistakes the workflow would never make.

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Neither one is better in the abstract. The workflow wins when the task is well defined.

17
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
The agent wins when flexibility and runtime decision-making genuinely matter at scale.

18
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Your job is matching the shape to the problem, not defaulting to the fancier one.

19
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Let's make the cost concrete because it surprises people. In a single call, you pay for one prompt

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
and one response. In an agent loop, the context doesn't reset between turns. Everything accumulates.

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
The original instructions, every tool call, and every result the model has seen so far. So turn

22
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
5 is carrying the weight of turns 1 through 4, and you pay for all of it again on each pass. Large

23
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
tool outputs are the biggest driver here. Dump a giant document into the loop, and it rides along

24
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
in every future turn. Latency stacks the same way, since each turn is another round trip to the model.

25
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:47,000
More turns means more money and more waiting. So where does the extra cost actually come from?

26
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Four places worth naming. More turns because the loop keeps calling the model until the task is

27
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,000
done, and each call is billable. Bigger context because every turn carries the full growing

28
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
history, so later turns are the most expensive ones. Compounding errors because a wrong step

29
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
early on feeds into every step after it, and the model builds on its own mistake. And slower

30
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:18,000
responses because sequential round trips add up into real wall clock time your user feels. None

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
of these are deal breakers. They're just the price of admission, and you should know you're paying it

32
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:29,000
before you commit to the loop. Compounding errors deserve their own moment because they're the

33
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:36,000
sneakiest cost. In a workflow, if a step misfires, it fails in a spot you can see, and your code can

34
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
catch it. In an agent, a small early mistake becomes an input to the next decision. The model

35
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:48,000
treats its own flawed output as fact and reasons forward from there. By a few turns later, it can

36
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:55,000
be confidently far off track with no obvious point of failure. This is why longer autonomous runs

37
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
carry more risk than short ones. The more steps the model takes without a checkpoint, the more

38
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
room a single wrong turn has to snowball. Bounding the loop and checking its work is how you keep

39
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:12,000
that in check. So what do you get by staying with a workflow? Three things that matter a lot

40
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,000
in production. Predictability because a fixed path produces the same behavior every run,

41
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:24,000
so you can reason about what will happen before it does. Auditability because you can trace exactly

42
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
which step ran in what order and why, which is priceless when something breaks, or a stakeholder

43
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,000
asks what happened. And consistency because there's no runtime improvisation to drift between calls.

44
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:42,000
These are the quiet advantages that don't show up in a demo but decide whether a system is

45
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
trustworthy at scale. When reliability is the priority, a deterministic design isn't the boring

46
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,000
choice. It's the correct one, and I'd reach for it first. Here's a habit that saves real pain.

47
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
When a problem could plausibly be either, build and validate the workflow version first.

48
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
It's faster to stand up, easier to test, and it gives you a baseline you actually understand.

49
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Run it against real inputs and see how far it gets. Often it gets far enough, and you've saved

50
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
yourself an agent you didn't need. When it falls short, you now know exactly where and why, which

51
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,000
tells you precisely which part needs the model's flexibility. That's a much stronger position than

52
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
starting with an open-ended agent and trying to rein it in later. Prove the simple thing first.

53
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Reach for autonomy only where the evidence says the workflow can't cope. The best real-world designs

54
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
are rarely all one thing. The most common hybrid puts an agent inside a single stage of a larger

55
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
workflow. Picture a fixed pipeline, validate the input, do the work, format the output. That middle

56
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
step, the work, might be genuinely open-ended. So you drop a scoped agent inside just that stage.

57
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Everything around it stays deterministic. The workflow around the agent handles ordering,

58
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:10,000
validation, and error handling. While the agent gets freedom only where flexibility earns its

59
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:17,000
cost, you get predictability at the edges and adaptability in the core. So don't think of this

60
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
as one choice for the whole system. Think of it as choosing, stage by stage, who owns each decision.

61
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:30,000
Here's the bottom line. An agent trades latency and token cost for flexibility that the model

62
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:37,000
brings at runtime. It matters because the trade only pays off for open-ended work. Just remember,

63
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:42,000
validate the workflow first and reach for the loop only when it can't cope on its own.

64
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:48,000
That wraps up the cost, latency, and reliability trade-offs. I'll see you in the next lesson.

